--- A text local written into a list by index (`xs[0] = first`) and read again
--- afterwards does not build compiled: the index assignment moves the local
--- although the IR does not mark that read as a move.
---
--- Interpreted (crow run -i): prints
---     b
---     ['b', 'b']
--- Compiled (crow run): the program does not build:
---     error[E0382]: use of moved value: `first`
---       --> src/main.rs:39:37
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused what Crow
---     accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program (`crow run -i` runs it meanwhile)
--- Expected: the same output as interpreted.
--- `crow build --ir` (inside a function, the same failure) is right:
---     let first: Str = xs[1]
---     xs[0] = first
---     display.log!held(move first)
--- but the emitter prints the index assignment as
---     { let _v = first; …; xs[_idx] = _v; }
--- which moves `first` where the IR reads it without `move` (the swap of two
--- items through a temporary, then a read of the temporary, is the usual shape).
xs = ['a', 'b']
first = xs[1]
xs[0] = first
log[first]
log[xs]
